welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is ned blackhawk a professor of history  and american studies at Yale his field  of interest is American Indian history  and he's the author of violence over the  land Indians and empires in the early  American West a study of the American  Great Basin that garnered numerous  professional prizes including the  Frederick Jackson Turner prize from the  Organization of American Historians is  also published numerous state of the  field essays from many of America's  leading professional historical  associations and has helped to build the  newly established Native American  cultural center here at Yale today we'll  talk with professor blackhawk about the  Yale group for the study of Native  America and his broader work to  institutionalize Native American Studies  welcome professor Black Hawk it's very  nice to be here let's start with an  overview of the Yale's group for the  study of Native America tell us about it  and its mission the yo group for the  study of Native America is a  interdisciplinary working group on  campus we refer to it as yyx know the  kind of acronym and it provides a  permanent space and a kind of community  for the city of Native America that  draws upon Yale's largely graduate  student but also faculty and staff  interest in this subject and what's its  mission our goal is to provide a  supportive inclusive and advanced  scholarly community for interested  participants who need or want to share  their work with a professional audience  of some form and to provide even more so  a kind of community around campus on  these issues draw  a draws from various professional  schools we have a lot of law students  various academic graduate programs and  also from an increasing number of  faculty on campus interested in these  subjects and how long has it been on  campus here it was founded in 2003 by a  group of concerned graduate students  that Yale like many other private  institutions at the time didn't have a  very prominent kind of scholarly profile  in the field of not just American Indian  history which is my field of study but  also Native American legal studies  Native American literary studies and the  graphic work art history and other types  of academic fields of inquiry these  students really kind of heroically built  a institutional space secured funding  for annual conferences and set in motion  some of the pressure on the  administration to begin the process of  hiring an academic faculty in the fields  and what are some of the projects that  you're working on with the group we're  doing lots of things so it's really  quite exciting  we run a bi-monthly working group that  meets in the newly established Native  American Cultural Center we showcase  emergent works in the field from some of  our graduate students who are finishing  dissertation chapters or research  projects doing initial kind of  publications we sponsor lots of academic  talks and events on campus this year  alone we've done two very large at least  in organization conferences that have  drawn collectively around a hundred  people to campus from across North  America on various kind of academically  themes subjects we also run a  dissertation of writing fellowship I'm  named after one of the most illustrious  early twentieth-century Native American  graduates named Henry Rho cloud ok this  fellowship brings visiting graduate  student to campus for the year to finish  the dissertation and is drawn from a  international pool of applicants and we  provide the kind of scholarly community  in which this fellow  does their academic work and then  presents it to a audience we also run  them not so much as a working group but  many of us are involved in other Native  American Studies efforts on campus and  at Yale including the running of a  University Press Series with the Yale in  American Indian studies okay are there a  number of these kinds of groups at  universities and colleges across the  country or not so many  well Yale is kind of a very active  academic community so it has lots of  comparable or heavily institutionalize  academic working communities there's  this famous one that you're probably  familiar with in agrarian studies run by  James Scott I have colleagues in the  history department who run sets of these  working groups so when I arrived here  about 6 or 7 years ago I began that well  I worked with people who were involved  in that earlier phases of this group and  helped secure kind of permanent  resources for it  so we're not unique here in a sense  except that we're the only group on  campus or does any American Studies but  we are in fact the only to my knowledge  professional academic working group of  its kind across any of the kind of  leading private institutions labor okay  that's wonderful it is but it's also  kind of sad in a sense that there aren't  that many other advanced communities in  this field right so the cultural center  tell us about that  what is it comprised of the Native  American cultural center is becoming one  of the most enriching places on campus I  think it's not I think it's not a unfair  to kind of use that very strong  characterization to describe this really  vibrant largely undergraduate student  community but very active and close  academic community on campus it's one of  the four cultural centers on campus that  is designed to provide a secure and  supportive community for  underrepresented students of color in  this case Native American students and  indigenous students from across largely  North America we have Alaska Native  students Native Hawaiian students and  nearly a record number of American  Indian undergraduates at the moment and  how many would that be  I think we're graduating fifty need  itself even if I knew the American  students in this year which is nearly  four percent of the entire student body  graduating class that's pretty amazing  actually it is and it reflects the kind  of growing institutional concern and  interest in these areas the center is  only two years old now so it has really  kind of state-of-the-art some in our  space and kitchen and other types of  amenities and one of the really most  exciting things is that there were once  only one or two really active Native  American student groups on campus and  now we have eight or nine Wow both  undergraduate and graduate students and  all of them generally use the center as  the site for their meetings in a lot of  their events and are they broken out by  basically different parts of the country  in terms of the Native American nation  that they're affiliated with less so  there are some at some institutions  there will be like a native Hawaiian  group or a Navajo student community club  or some kind of Association these groups  are kind of organized around student  interests and things like health or our  law students have a group or our Native  American graduate students have an  active group the working group is  another group that's affiliated in part  with the center but the undergraduates  also have a drumming group  so it's more it's more than just  centered around the artistic part of  Native Americans correct it's really a  academic as well as scholarly so where  would you like to see that the cultural  center go in the next five years it'd be  really nice to see a few more additional  Native American faculty on campus are  you the only one at this moment I'm in  college and it'd be really nice to see  some more kind of collaborative  partnerships between campus and other  Native American either institutional  programs or even communities themselves  on Indian country you've done a few  really interesting collaborations with  the Mohegan nation here in Connecticut  we've had events at the Pequot museum  also and I've recently hired a few  Native American community leaders from  across campus to work with us in various  ways okay let's talk about your academic  work you recently completed an anthology  with Isaiah Willner called indigenous  visions rediscovering the world of Franz  boas let's tell us about Franz Boas who  who he is and then let's talk a little  bit about the anthology and what it grew  out of the legacy of anthropology in the  field of American Indian studies is  really hard to overestimate or  overemphasize anthropology is a kind of  scholarly field emerged in the late 19th  early 20th century and really had as its  primary subjects of inquiry indigenous  communities largely in North America and  the kind of leading proponent or the  kind of institutional founder of that  discipline was a German ethnologist by  the name of Franz boas who migrated out  of Germany in the late 18-hundreds and  traveled around North America  and eventually helped organize like  incredibly extensive cultural  exhibitions like at the World's Fair in  Chicago in 1893 and eventually worked  for decades in New York City  institutionalizing the discipline of  anthropology at Columbia University and  also building like the New York Sun  Natural History Museum and and then  training dozens and dozens of a future  anthropologists many of whom would study  native languages and begin recording  what they thought were disappearing  cultural practices and really helped  establish a kind of academic vernacular  and institutional understanding of the  peoples of the world in which Native  Americans were usually you had the  sentiment or the primary  ethnographically identifiable other to  Western societies emergent in the sense  of its own subjectivity so we have this  very robust now community of graduate  students here one of them is writing a  academic dissertation on not just boas  but the particularly the Native American  communities that shaped and informed him  and he very relatively early on in his  graduate training decided he'd like to  do a big conference and so using our  yyyx networking group and working with  macmillan where we held the the event we  brought together a dozens of scholars  for a few days and really interrogated  the relative place not only of Boaz as a  kind of ethnographic influencer of  subsequent scholarly debates but  particularly those who shaped him okay  so the world around him and we came to  release kind of firm understanding that  we shouldn't think of these intellectual  leaders and certain scholarly fields as  being singular figures but having been  deeply imprinted by particularly their  native informants so we spent quite a  bit of time working with these visiting  scholars and  soliciting extended papers from them and  have compiled them into a volume that  really rethinks the legacy of not just  in a sense boas but really of American  Anthropological thought and so what are  some of the conclusions that came out of  it well a kind of primacy of one of the  kind of primary conclusions is that the  world in which anthropologists were  forming their ideas about native peoples  was not something that they alone were  constructing and that there are many  attempts both here and also globally  where various ethnographic informants  were trying to in a sense articulate a  certain social or kind of cultural or  even political vision through the  discourse of ethnography in a sense have  the ethnographer understand their  concerns and potentially bring them to  other colonial or Imperial officials or  help redress the levels of say cultural  censorship that they were experiencing  so boaz it was most famous for working  among the Northwest coasts indigenous  communities particularly in British  Columbia and those communities at the  time he was there and at the time he  brought them or helped bring them to  Chicago were undergoing very heavy  persecution of their cultural and  religious practices and so they saw in  him an ally who could help articulate a  vision of their cultural organization to  outsiders at a time when government  officials and missionaries and various  kind of institutions of the state were  heavily prosecuting them so we're  looking at the kind of dynamic agency  that indigenous peoples have been able  to deploy at various times through these  sets of ethnographic encounters and how  do you think that would apply to Native  America today well I think it's relevant  to think of indigenous peoples as having  been very active participants in the  making of both the intellectual  structures that have historically under  recognized them  as well as the larger societies around  them so the idea that museums or  scholarly text books or children's  literature convey that these peoples are  without history or primitive or somehow  inherently different is in fact the kind  of message that native peoples have been  contesting for generations  thank you so much for being here with us  today and sharing some of your work it's  been a pleasure for more information  about Professor Black Hawk and his  research please visit our website at  macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you

